he IUPAC condensed glycan symbol set. No supplied citation contradicts any part of the claim; there is no counter-assignment of Neu5Ac, GlcNAc, GalNAc, Man or Fuc to a different monosaccharide anywhere in the packet. The submission's own limitations note flags a divergence rather than a contradiction: KEGG classifies Neu5Ac under amino sugars rather than under a 'sialic acid' label, and ChEBI frames fucose as a deoxyhexose — these are classification-vocabulary differences that neither confirm nor refute the claim's name mappings, but they do leave the 'sialic acid' parenthetical uncorroborated. One further reference (Essentials of Glycobiology, NBK579927) is pinned but unquoted and therefore carries no evidentiary weight here.","Uncertainty and limits of a documentary conclusion: This assessment compares claim text to quoted source text only; it establishes nothing about whether the skill actually returns these names when given these abbreviations. The packet itself records that the subject skill was never executed for this claim and that the required grade-critique step failed three times in the run, so there is no behavioural or performance evidence whatsoever — the 'expected_behavior' (returning the tabulated full name on input of an abbreviation) is entirely untested. Even for the three supported pairings, the evidence type is synonym co-listing in a compound registry, which shows the abbreviation is a recognised alias but does not itself verify a lookup implementation, its coverage, its handling of stereochemical qualifiers (D-/L- prefixes appear in the sources but not in the claim's names), or its behaviour on inputs outside the five listed. The two unevidenced pairings (Man, Fuc) are widely conventional in glycan notation, but conventional familiarity is not documentary support; absence of a citation here yields insufficiency, not falsity. Accordingly the correct documentary verdict is that support is incomplete rather than that the claim is wrong."],"citatio